IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `gva-dates`

-- Graffiti Vandalism Act 2016 (WA) -- the seat-neutral core.
--
-- The Act's operative provisions in its own actors and terms: "a person", "the offender", "the owner", "the
-- occupier", "the notice recipient" (s 19(3)), "the affected person" (s 20), "the local government". Duties,
-- prohibitions and permissions are regulative rules (DEONTIC), ten in all: ss 5, 6, 7, 18, 19(3)-(4), 22, 23, 24, 25
-- and 27-28. Penalties, the minimum penalties, the s 18(5) daily further fine, cost recovery and the court's power to
-- apportion are functions.
--
-- The local government is an actor here: it gives notices, may obliterate graffiti, may enter land. It is never a
-- seat. The questions people ask are in gva-seat.l4, which IMPORTs this module and restates no rule.
--
-- Judgments the Act leaves to someone else -- the local government's view that graffiti is "unsightly or offensive",
-- or that it was obliterated "in a manner acceptable to the local government", a "reasonable excuse", circumstances
-- giving rise to a "reasonable suspicion", belief on "reasonable grounds", "gross negligence", what the court
-- "considers fair and reasonable" -- are inputs, never guessed.
--
-- Time on the regulative rules' timeline is whole days, as day numbers (gva-dates.l4), except the entry rule (ss 27-28),
-- which counts minutes from the notice of entry.
--
-- Edition: the compilation at legislation.wa.gov.au (law_a147110, mrdoc 29645), retrieved 7 September 2026,
-- including the Local Government Legislation Amendment Act 2016 (No. 26 of 2016). Deposited at
-- ../../registers/source-bundle/mrdoc_29645.txt. Based on content from the Western Australian Legislation website at
-- 2026-09-07, "Graffiti Vandalism Act 2016", State of Western Australia, licensed under CC BY 4.0 (see SOURCE-LICENSE.md).
--
-- BASELINE. See NOTES.md. Not for public use as advice.

§ `Graffiti Vandalism Act 2016 (WA) -- seat-neutral core`

§§ `The parties and acts of the regulative rules`

DECLARE `Graffiti party` IS ONE OF
    `the person`                          -- anyone at all: ss 5, 6 ("A person must not ...")
    `the seller`                          -- the person who sells: s 7
    `the notice recipient`                -- s 19(3): the person given a notice under s 18
    `the affected person`                 -- s 20: the notice recipient, and the owner if the recipient is not the owner
    `the local government`                -- the local government that gave the notice, or whose district it is

-- s 18(2): the graffiti is to be obliterated "in a manner acceptable to the local government". Whether it was is the
-- local government's judgment, so it is part of the act: an obliteration it does not accept does not count.
DECLARE `Manner of obliteration` IS ONE OF
    `in a manner acceptable to the local government`
    `in a manner the local government does not accept`

DECLARE `Graffiti act` IS ONE OF
    `put the mark on the property`                                                     -- s 5
    `be in possession of the thing`                                                    -- s 6
    `sell the item to the buyer`                                                       -- s 7
    `ensure the graffiti is obliterated` HAS `the manner` IS A `Manner of obliteration` -- s 18(2)
    `obliterate the graffiti`                                                          -- ss 19(3), 24, 25 (the local government)
    `pay the local government the reasonable cost of obliterating the graffiti`        -- s 19(4)
    `lodge an objection in the approved form with the CEO of the local government`     -- s 22(2)
    `allow further time to object`                                                     -- s 22(2)
    `apply to the State Administrative Tribunal for a review`                          -- s 23
    `ask the State Administrative Tribunal to extend the time for applying`            -- SAT Act 2004 s 92
    `enter the land`                                                                   -- ss 27-29

§§ `Part 1 -- s 3 Terms used`

-- s 3: "graffiti means any drawing, writing, painting, symbol or mark applied to or marked on property by -- (a)
-- spraying, writing, drawing, marking or otherwise applying paint or another marking substance; or (b) scratching or
-- etching". A poster or sticker stuck on is not graffiti under the Act.
DECLARE `How the mark was applied` IS ONE OF
    `by spraying, writing, drawing, marking or otherwise applying paint or another marking substance`
    `by scratching or etching`
    `in some other way, such as sticking on a poster or sticker`

GIVEN `how it was applied` IS A `How the mark was applied`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 3 -- the mark is graffiti` IF
    CONSIDER `how it was applied`
    WHEN `by spraying, writing, drawing, marking or otherwise applying paint or another marking substance` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `by scratching or etching` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

§§ `Part 2 -- s 4 Terms used`

-- s 4: "adult means a person who has reached 18 years of age"; "child means a person under 18 years of age".
`s 4 -- the age at which a person is an adult` MEANS 18

GIVEN `the age` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 4 -- a person of this age is a child` IF
    `the age` LESS THAN `s 4 -- the age at which a person is an adult`

-- s 4: "graffiti implement means any of these -- (a) a can of spray paint; (b) a pen or marker pen, or a similar
-- implement that -- (i) has a tip over 6 mm wide; and (ii) contains a fluid that is not water soluble and that is
-- capable of marking a surface".
DECLARE `Kind of item` IS ONE OF
    `a can of spray paint`
    `a pen or marker pen, or a similar implement`
    `something else`

`s 4 -- the tip width a pen must be over, in millimetres` MEANS 6

GIVEN `the kind of item` IS A `Kind of item`
      `the width of its tip in millimetres` IS A NUMBER
      `it contains a fluid that is not water soluble and is capable of marking a surface` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 4 -- the item is a graffiti implement` IF
    CONSIDER `the kind of item`
    WHEN `a can of spray paint` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a pen or marker pen, or a similar implement` THEN
            `the width of its tip in millimetres` GREATER THAN `s 4 -- the tip width a pen must be over, in millimetres`
        AND `it contains a fluid that is not water soluble and is capable of marking a surface`
    OTHERWISE FALSE

§§ `s 5 Damaging property by graffiti`

-- s 5(1): "A person must not destroy, damage or deface the property of another person by graffiti without that
-- other person's consent." s 5(2): property "includes vegetation", so a tree, hedge or garden is property here.
-- Reading: putting graffiti on property defaces it, so the elements are a mark that is graffiti (s 3), on another
-- person's property, without that person's consent.
GIVEN `how it was applied` IS A `How the mark was applied`
      `the property is another person's` IS A BOOLEAN
      `that person consented` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 5(1) -- putting the mark on the property is an offence` IF
        `s 3 -- the mark is graffiti` `how it was applied`
    AND `the property is another person's`
    AND (NOT `that person consented`)

-- s 5(1) Penalty: "a fine of $24 000 and imprisonment for 2 years". These are maximums (Sentencing Act 1995 s 9(2)),
-- and "and" lets them be imposed alternatively or together (s 9(6)); both deposited, not encoded.
`s 5(1) -- the maximum fine` MEANS 24000
`s 5(1) -- the maximum imprisonment, in years` MEANS 2

-- s 5(1) Penalty: "but the minimum penalty -- (a) for an adult offender, is a community based order; (b) for a child
-- offender, is a youth community based order." s 5(3): this operates despite the Young Offenders Act 1994 s 46(5a).
-- Reading: adult or child as at the time of the offence (the Act does not say; NOTES.md finding 7).
DECLARE `Minimum penalty under s 5` IS ONE OF
    `a community based order under the Sentencing Act 1995`
    `a youth community based order under the Young Offenders Act 1994`

GIVEN `the offender's age` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A `Minimum penalty under s 5`
`s 5(1)(a), (b) -- the minimum penalty` MEANS
    IF `s 4 -- a person of this age is a child` `the offender's age`
    THEN `a youth community based order under the Young Offenders Act 1994`
    ELSE `a community based order under the Sentencing Act 1995`

-- The prohibition itself. Where the facts make it an offence, the act is forbidden and doing it is a breach; where
-- they do not (the mark is not graffiti, the property is the person's own, or the owner consented), this Act does not
-- forbid it. A prohibition with no time limit: it has no WITHIN, so time alone never ends it.
GIVEN `how it was applied` IS A `How the mark was applied`
      `the property is another person's` IS A BOOLEAN
      `that person consented` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Graffiti party` `Graffiti act`
`s 5(1) -- a person must not damage another person's property by graffiti without consent` MEANS
    IF `s 5(1) -- putting the mark on the property is an offence` `how it was applied` `the property is another person's` `that person consented`
    THEN PARTY `the person`
         SHANT `put the mark on the property`
         LEST  BREACH BY `the person` BECAUSE "s 5(1): a person must not destroy, damage or deface the property of another person by graffiti without that person's consent; penalty a fine of $24 000 and imprisonment for 2 years, with a minimum penalty of a community based order (adult) or a youth community based order (child)"
    ELSE PARTY `the person`
         MAY   `put the mark on the property`

§§ `s 6 Possessing thing with intent to apply graffiti`

-- s 6(1): "A person must not be in possession of a thing with the intention of using it to destroy, damage or
-- deface property by applying graffiti." s 6(2): the person "is presumed to have" that intention "if the person is in
-- possession of the thing in circumstances that give rise to a reasonable suspicion that the person has the
-- intention, unless the contrary is proved". The suspicion and the proof are for the court: inputs.
-- s 4: possession "includes having under control in any manner whatever ... although another person has the actual
-- possession or custody".
GIVEN `the person intends to use the thing to apply graffiti` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the circumstances give rise to a reasonable suspicion that the person has that intention` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person proves that they did not have that intention` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 6(1), (2) -- the person has, or is presumed to have, the intention` IF
        `the person intends to use the thing to apply graffiti`
    OR (    `the circumstances give rise to a reasonable suspicion that the person has that intention`
        AND (NOT `the person proves that they did not have that intention`))

-- s 6(1) Penalty: "a fine of $6 000".
`s 6(1) -- the maximum fine` MEANS 6000

GIVEN `the person intends to use the thing to apply graffiti` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the circumstances give rise to a reasonable suspicion that the person has that intention` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person proves that they did not have that intention` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Graffiti party` `Graffiti act`
`s 6(1) -- a person must not possess a thing with the intention of applying graffiti with it` MEANS
    IF `s 6(1), (2) -- the person has, or is presumed to have, the intention` `the person intends to use the thing to apply graffiti` `the circumstances give rise to a reasonable suspicion that the person has that intention` `the person proves that they did not have that intention`
    THEN PARTY `the person`
         SHANT `be in possession of the thing`
         LEST  BREACH BY `the person` BECAUSE "s 6(1), (2): a person must not be in possession of a thing with the intention of using it to apply graffiti, an intention presumed from circumstances giving rise to a reasonable suspicion unless the contrary is proved; penalty a fine of $6 000"
    ELSE PARTY `the person`
         MAY   `be in possession of the thing`

§§ `s 7 Selling graffiti implement to child`

-- s 7(1): "A person must not sell a graffiti implement to a child."
GIVEN `the kind of item` IS A `Kind of item`
      `the width of its tip in millimetres` IS A NUMBER
      `it contains a fluid that is not water soluble and is capable of marking a surface` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the buyer's age` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 7(1) -- selling the item to the buyer is an offence` IF
        `s 4 -- the item is a graffiti implement` `the kind of item` `the width of its tip in millimetres` `it contains a fluid that is not water soluble and is capable of marking a surface`
    AND `s 4 -- a person of this age is a child` `the buyer's age`

-- s 7(1) Penalty: "(a) for a first offence, a fine of $6 000; (b) for a subsequent offence, a fine of $12 000."
-- Reading: a subsequent offence is one after a conviction for an earlier s 7(1) offence (the Act does not define it;
-- NOTES.md finding 6).
`s 7(1)(a) -- the maximum fine for a first offence` MEANS 6000
`s 7(1)(b) -- the maximum fine for a subsequent offence` MEANS 12000

GIVEN `the seller has been convicted of an offence under s 7(1) before` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 7(1) -- the maximum fine` MEANS
    IF `the seller has been convicted of an offence under s 7(1) before`
    THEN `s 7(1)(b) -- the maximum fine for a subsequent offence`
    ELSE `s 7(1)(a) -- the maximum fine for a first offence`

-- s 7(2): "It is a defence ... to prove the accused, or a person acting on behalf of the accused, believed on
-- reasonable grounds that the person to whom the implement was sold was an adult." Whether the grounds were
-- reasonable is for the court: an input. The seller has to prove it.
GIVEN `the seller, or a person acting for the seller, believed the buyer was an adult` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the belief was on reasonable grounds` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 7(2) -- the seller has a defence` IF
        `the seller, or a person acting for the seller, believed the buyer was an adult`
    AND `the belief was on reasonable grounds`

-- The prohibition, with its defence. Selling a graffiti implement to a child is forbidden whatever the seller
-- believed; the s 7(2) defence answers the charge. So the defence sits in the LEST: where it is proved, the sale leads
-- to no breach and nothing more is owed (FULFILLED here means that, not that the buyer was an adult); where it is not,
-- the sale is a breach, the offence.
GIVEN `the kind of item` IS A `Kind of item`
      `the width of its tip in millimetres` IS A NUMBER
      `it contains a fluid that is not water soluble and is capable of marking a surface` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the buyer's age` IS A NUMBER
      `the seller, or a person acting for the seller, believed the buyer was an adult` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the belief was on reasonable grounds` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Graffiti party` `Graffiti act`
`s 7 -- a person must not sell a graffiti implement to a child, unless they believed on reasonable grounds the buyer was an adult` MEANS
    IF `s 7(1) -- selling the item to the buyer is an offence` `the kind of item` `the width of its tip in millimetres` `it contains a fluid that is not water soluble and is capable of marking a surface` `the buyer's age`
    THEN PARTY `the seller`
         SHANT `sell the item to the buyer`
         LEST  IF `s 7(2) -- the seller has a defence` `the seller, or a person acting for the seller, believed the buyer was an adult` `the belief was on reasonable grounds`
               THEN FULFILLED
               ELSE BREACH BY `the seller` BECAUSE "s 7(1): a person must not sell a graffiti implement to a child; penalty a fine of $6 000 for a first offence and $12 000 for a subsequent offence (the s 7(2) defence, a belief on reasonable grounds that the buyer was an adult, not proved)"
    ELSE PARTY `the seller`
         MAY   `sell the item to the buyer`

§§ `ss 8-10, 13 What the court may or must order on conviction`

-- s 8(1): the section applies whether the graffiti is visible to the public or not, on public or private property.
-- s 8(2): a court that convicts a person of an offence under s 5 "may order the offender -- (a) to take remedial
-- action to restore the property ...; or (b) to pay another person to take remedial action". s 9 applies the
-- Sentencing Act 1995's reparation provisions to the order (not encoded). Only a s 5 conviction: not s 6 or s 7.
GIVEN `the person has been convicted of an offence under s 5` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 8(2) -- the court may order the offender to restore the property, or to pay another person to` IF
    `the person has been convicted of an offence under s 5`

-- s 10(1): "Where an offender is sentenced to a community order, the court must impose a condition that a minimum of
-- 10 hours unpaid community work be carried out". s 10(2): the work is "to comprise graffiti removal work, where
-- practicable", whether the offender is an adult or a child. "offender" is a person who has committed an offence
-- under s 5 (s 4), so this is for s 5 only.
`s 10(1) -- the minimum hours of unpaid community work in a community order` MEANS 10

-- s 13(1): The Criminal Code s 731 (deposited, not encoded) applies on conviction of an offence under this Act: the
-- court "may make an order for the forfeiture to the State, or the destruction or disposal" of a thing used in or in
-- connection with the offence (s 731(1)), once the owner has had the opportunity to show cause (s 731(2)).
-- s 13(2): the things include "a thing that was intended to be used, and ... (a) a graffiti implement; and (b)
-- anything used by the offender to record, store or transmit images of the graffiti caused by the offender".
-- s 13(4): the court "must not make an order for the forfeiture of any thing ... unless the prosecutor applies".
-- So the answer is that the court may: whether it does is its discretion under s 731.
DECLARE `Thing that may be forfeited` IS ONE OF
    `a graffiti implement`
    `a thing the offender used to record, store or send images of the graffiti they caused`
    `another thing used, or intended to be used, in or in connection with the offence`
    `a thing not used or intended to be used in connection with the offence`

GIVEN `the thing` IS A `Thing that may be forfeited`
      `the prosecutor has applied for its forfeiture` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 13 -- the court may order the thing forfeited on conviction` IF
        `s 13(2) -- the thing is within s 731`
    AND `the prosecutor has applied for its forfeiture`
    WHERE
        `s 13(2) -- the thing is within s 731` MEANS
            CONSIDER `the thing`
            WHEN `a graffiti implement` THEN TRUE
            WHEN `a thing the offender used to record, store or send images of the graffiti they caused` THEN TRUE
            WHEN `another thing used, or intended to be used, in or in connection with the offence` THEN TRUE
            OTHERWISE FALSE

§§ `s 12 Immunity for owners of property`

-- s 12(2): "An action in tort does not lie against the owner of a place for death or injury suffered by an
-- individual resulting from the individual's involvement in graffiti removal under this Act or under a community
-- order, unless a breach of the owner's duty of care amounts to gross negligence." s 12(3): the action may be taken
-- against the State instead. Gross negligence is for the court: an input.
GIVEN `a breach of the owner's duty of care amounts to gross negligence` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 12(2) -- an action in tort lies against the owner` IF
    `a breach of the owner's duty of care amounts to gross negligence`

§§ `Part 3 Division 2 -- s 18 Notice requiring removal of graffiti`

-- s 18(2): the notice goes to "a person who is the owner of property or the occupier of a place". "owner" is the Local
-- Government Act 1995 s 1.4 owner (s 3), "occupier" the s 1.4 occupier (s 14): asked as facts.
DECLARE `Owner or occupier` IS ONE OF
    `the owner`
    `the occupier, and not the owner`
    `both the owner and the occupier`

-- s 18(1): "This section applies to graffiti that is -- (a) applied to property with the consent of the owner or
-- occupier; and (b) visible from a public place; and (c) considered by the local government to be unsightly or
-- offensive." (c) is the local government's judgment: an input. "public place" is defined in s 14 (a gloss in the
-- seat's @desc).
GIVEN `the graffiti was applied with the consent of the owner or occupier` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the graffiti is visible from a public place` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the local government considers the graffiti unsightly or offensive` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 18(1) -- section 18 applies to the graffiti` IF
        `the graffiti was applied with the consent of the owner or occupier`
    AND `the graffiti is visible from a public place`
    AND `the local government considers the graffiti unsightly or offensive`

-- s 18(2): the local government "may give a notice in writing to a person who is the owner of property or the
-- occupier of a place on which graffiti described in subsection (1) is applied, requiring the person to ensure that
-- the graffiti is obliterated in a manner acceptable to the local government within a time set out in the notice."
-- The duty exists only once such a notice is given, and only for graffiti s 18(1) describes. s 18(4): the terms on
-- which land is occupied do not prevent compliance.
GIVEN `a notice in writing under s 18(2) has been given to the person` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the graffiti was applied with the consent of the owner or occupier` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the graffiti is visible from a public place` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the local government considers the graffiti unsightly or offensive` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 18(1), (2) -- the person must ensure the graffiti is obliterated` IF
        `a notice in writing under s 18(2) has been given to the person`
    AND `s 18(1) -- section 18 applies to the graffiti` `the graffiti was applied with the consent of the owner or occupier` `the graffiti is visible from a public place` `the local government considers the graffiti unsightly or offensive`

-- s 18(3): "If the notice is given to an occupier of land who is not the owner of the property, the owner is to be
-- informed in writing that the notice was given."
GIVEN `the notice recipient is` IS AN `Owner or occupier`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 18(3) -- the owner is to be informed in writing that the notice was given` IF
    CONSIDER `the notice recipient is`
    WHEN `the occupier, and not the owner` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

-- s 18(5) Penalty: "(a) a fine of $5 000; (b) a further fine of $500 in respect of each day or part of a day during
-- which the offence continues." Sentencing Act 1995 s 9(7) (deposited): a daily penalty "may be imposed for each day or
-- part of a day during which the offence continues, in addition to any other penalty".
`s 18(5)(a) -- the maximum fine` MEANS 5000
`s 18(5)(b) -- the further fine for each day or part of a day the offence continues` MEANS 500

GIVEN `the days or part days the offence has continued` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 18(5) -- the maximum fine, with the further fine` MEANS
    `s 18(5)(a) -- the maximum fine` PLUS (`s 18(5)(b) -- the further fine for each day or part of a day the offence continues` TIMES `the days or part days the offence has continued`)

-- The last day for compliance: the time set out in the notice, counted from the day the notice is given. The same
-- `the days the notice allows` is the WITHIN of the rule below.
GIVEN `the day the notice was given` IS A DATE, `the days the notice allows` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`s 18(2) -- the last day to comply with the notice` MEANS
    `the days the notice allows` `days after` `the day the notice was given`

-- Reading of "each day or part of a day during which the offence continues": the offence is committed when the time
-- in the notice runs out, and continues on each later day until the graffiti is obliterated, counting the day it is
-- obliterated as a part day. So obliterating it 5 days after the last day is 5 days (NOTES.md finding 5).
GIVEN `the day the notice was given` IS A DATE
      `the days the notice allows` IS A NUMBER
      `the day the graffiti was obliterated, or the day the count is made` IS A DATE
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 18(5)(b) -- the days or part days after the last day to comply` MEANS
    IF `the days after the last day` GREATER THAN 0
    THEN `the days after the last day`
    ELSE 0
    WHERE
        `the days after the last day` MEANS
            `the days from` (`s 18(2) -- the last day to comply with the notice` `the day the notice was given` `the days the notice allows`) `to` `the day the graffiti was obliterated, or the day the count is made`

-- The duty. Timeline in days, starting on the day the notice is given. s 18(5): failing to comply "without a
-- reasonable excuse" is an offence, so the breach is what follows only where there is no reasonable excuse. Where there
-- is one, there is no offence, but the local government's s 19(3) power arises all the same ("fails to comply with it
-- for any reason"). Without an excuse, the breach carries the offence and, in its words, the s 19 consequences (a
-- BREACH ends the trace, so the s 19 rule cannot be chained after it: NOTES.md finding 22).
GIVEN `the days the notice allows` IS A NUMBER
      `the notice recipient has a reasonable excuse` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Graffiti party` `Graffiti act`
`s 18(2), (5) -- the notice recipient must ensure the graffiti is obliterated within the time in the notice` MEANS
    PARTY `the notice recipient`
    MUST  `ensure the graffiti is obliterated` `in a manner acceptable to the local government`
    WITHIN `the days the notice allows`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST  IF `the notice recipient has a reasonable excuse`
          THEN `s 19(3), (4) -- the local government may obliterate the graffiti and recover its reasonable cost`
          ELSE BREACH BY `the notice recipient` BECAUSE "s 18(5): failing to comply with the notice without a reasonable excuse is an offence, penalty a fine of $5 000 and a further fine of $500 for each day or part of a day during which the offence continues; and under s 19(3)-(4) the local government may do anything it considers necessary to obliterate the graffiti and recover its reasonable cost as a debt"

§§ `s 19 Additional powers when notice is given`

-- s 19(3): "If the person who is given the notice (the notice recipient) fails to comply with it for any reason, the
-- local government may do anything that it considers necessary to obliterate the graffiti". s 19(4): it "may recover
-- the reasonable cost of anything it does under subsection (3) as a debt due from the person who failed to comply".
-- No time is set for either: the rule has no WITHIN.
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Graffiti party` `Graffiti act`
`s 19(3), (4) -- the local government may obliterate the graffiti and recover its reasonable cost` MEANS
    PARTY `the local government`
    MAY   `obliterate the graffiti`
    HENCE
        PARTY `the notice recipient`
        MUST  `pay the local government the reasonable cost of obliterating the graffiti`

-- s 19(5): a notice recipient may apply to a court for an order under s 19(6) if it "(a) incurs expense in complying
-- ...; or (b) fails to comply ... and, as a consequence, is fined or has to pay to a local government the cost it
-- incurs". s 19(6): the court may order "(a) if the notice recipient is the owner -- the occupier; or (b) if the
-- notice recipient is the occupier -- the owner, to pay ... so much of that expense, fine or cost as the court
-- considers fair and reasonable". s 19(7): the court has regard to the type of land, the terms of occupation, and
-- any other relevant matter. How much is for the court.
DECLARE `Who the court may order to pay the notice recipient` IS ONE OF
    `the occupier`
    `the owner`
    `no one, because the notice recipient is both the owner and the occupier`
    `no one, because the notice recipient has no expense, fine or cost of the kind in s 19(5)`

GIVEN `the notice recipient is` IS AN `Owner or occupier`
      `the notice recipient incurred expense complying, or was fined or had to pay the local government's cost` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Who the court may order to pay the notice recipient`
`s 19(5), (6) -- who the court may order to pay the notice recipient` MEANS
    IF (NOT `the notice recipient incurred expense complying, or was fined or had to pay the local government's cost`)
    THEN `no one, because the notice recipient has no expense, fine or cost of the kind in s 19(5)`
    ELSE CONSIDER `the notice recipient is`
         WHEN `the owner` THEN `the occupier`
         WHEN `the occupier, and not the owner` THEN `the owner`
         WHEN `both the owner and the occupier` THEN `no one, because the notice recipient is both the owner and the occupier`

§§ `Part 3 Division 3 -- ss 20-24 Objection to a notice`

-- s 20: "If the person to whom a local government gives a notice under section 18 is not the owner of the property to
-- which the notice relates, the owner is also an affected person". An occupier is not added when the notice goes to
-- the owner.
DECLARE `Who the notice was given to` IS ONE OF
    `to the person`
    `to the occupier of the person's property, and the person is its owner`
    `to someone else, or no notice has been given`

GIVEN `the notice was given` IS A `Who the notice was given to`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 20 -- the person is an affected person` IF
    CONSIDER `the notice was given`
    WHEN `to the person` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `to the occupier of the person's property, and the person is its owner` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

-- s 21: whenever a notice is given, the person giving it "is to ensure that, as soon as practicable after the notice is
-- given, the affected person is given written reasons for being given the notice and is informed of the person's
-- rights under this Division to object against, and apply for a review of, the decision to give the notice." No time
-- in days is set ("as soon as practicable"), so this is a function, not a rule with a WITHIN.
GIVEN `the notice was given` IS A `Who the notice was given to`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 21 -- the person is to be given written reasons and told of the rights to object and to apply for review` IF
    `s 20 -- the person is an affected person` `the notice was given`

-- s 22(1): "An affected person may object to a notice if the person has not applied under this Division for a review
-- of the decision to give the notice."
GIVEN `the notice was given` IS A `Who the notice was given to`
      `the person has applied to the State Administrative Tribunal for a review of the decision to give the notice` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 22(1) -- the person may object to the notice` IF
        `s 20 -- the person is an affected person` `the notice was given`
    AND (NOT `the person has applied to the State Administrative Tribunal for a review of the decision to give the notice`)

-- s 22(2): the objection is lodged with the chief executive officer of the local government "within 28 days after the
-- notice is given, or within such further time as the local government may allow". Reading: further time runs on
-- from the end of the 28 days. The Interpretation Act 1984 s 64(1) (deposited) lets the further time be allowed even
-- after the 28 days have run out.
`s 22(2) -- the days to object after the notice is given` MEANS 28

GIVEN `the further days the local government has allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 22(2) -- the days to object` MEANS
    `s 22(2) -- the days to object after the notice is given` PLUS `the further days the local government has allowed`

GIVEN `the day the notice was given` IS A DATE, `the further days the local government has allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`s 22(2) -- the last day to object` MEANS
    (`s 22(2) -- the days to object` `the further days the local government has allowed`) `days after` `the day the notice was given`

-- The permission. Written with DO, not MAY: on the stable l4 a MAY takes its HENCE both when it is used and when its
-- time runs out (NOTES.md finding 21), so a trace of a MAY cannot tell an objection lodged in time from one lodged
-- late. DO takes HENCE when the objection is lodged in time, and LEST when the time runs out; the LEST is no breach
-- (not objecting is no wrong), only what the text leaves: the local government may still allow further time.
GIVEN `the further days the local government has allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Graffiti party` `Graffiti act`
`s 22(2) -- the affected person may object within 28 days after the notice is given, or any further time allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `the affected person`
    DO    `lodge an objection in the approved form with the CEO of the local government`
    WITHIN `s 22(2) -- the days to object` `the further days the local government has allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST  PARTY `the local government`
          MAY   `allow further time to object`

-- s 23(1): an affected person may apply to the State Administrative Tribunal for a review of the decision to give the
-- notice if the person "(a) has not lodged an objection to the notice; or (b) has lodged an objection but, at the
-- expiration of 35 days after it was lodged, has not been given notice in writing of how it has been decided to
-- dispose of the objection." s 23(2): once given that notice, "the person may apply ... for a review of the decision on
-- the objection". Worked out from the facts: whether an objection was lodged, whether written notice of the decision
-- has been given, and how many days have passed since lodging. Where written notice comes after day 35, the (2) route
-- is taken (NOTES.md finding 3).
DECLARE `The right to apply for review` IS ONE OF
    `s 23(1)(a) -- no objection has been lodged`
    `s 23(1)(b) -- an objection was lodged and no written decision was given within 35 days`
    `s 23(2) -- the objection has been decided and written notice of the decision given`
    `none yet -- an objection was lodged, is undecided, and 35 days have not yet passed`

`s 23(1)(b) -- the days after lodging an objection before the right arises` MEANS 35

GIVEN `an objection has been lodged` IS A BOOLEAN
      `written notice of how the objection was decided has been given` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the days since the objection was lodged` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A `The right to apply for review`
`s 23(1), (2) -- the right to apply for review` MEANS
    IF (NOT `an objection has been lodged`)
    THEN `s 23(1)(a) -- no objection has been lodged`
    ELSE IF `written notice of how the objection was decided has been given`
         THEN `s 23(2) -- the objection has been decided and written notice of the decision given`
         ELSE IF `the days since the objection was lodged` GREATER THAN `s 23(1)(b) -- the days after lodging an objection before the right arises`
              THEN `s 23(1)(b) -- an objection was lodged and no written decision was given within 35 days`
              ELSE `none yet -- an objection was lodged, is undecided, and 35 days have not yet passed`

GIVEN `the right` IS A `The right to apply for review`
      `the notice was given` IS A `Who the notice was given to`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 23(1), (2) -- the person may apply to the State Administrative Tribunal` IF
        `s 20 -- the person is an affected person` `the notice was given`
    AND `there is a right to apply`
    WHERE
        `there is a right to apply` MEANS
            CONSIDER `the right`
            WHEN `none yet -- an objection was lodged, is undecided, and 35 days have not yet passed` THEN FALSE
            OTHERWISE TRUE

-- s 23(3): "The application is to be made within 42 days after the right to make it arose which is -- (a) within 42
-- days after the notice is given ...; or (b) more than 35 days, but within 77 days, after the objection was lodged ...;
-- or (c) within 42 days after the objection was decided". The chapeau sets one period, 42 days after the right arose,
-- and the paragraphs say when each right arises: (a) the day of the notice, (b) the end of the 35th day after
-- lodging (so 35 + 42 = 77), (c) the day the objection was decided (NOTES.md finding 1: the chapeau and (c) can
-- differ). While an objection is undecided and 35 days have not passed, the (b) window is the one ahead.
`s 23(3) -- the days to apply after the right arose` MEANS 42

GIVEN `the right` IS A `The right to apply for review`
      `the day the notice was given` IS A DATE
      `the day the objection was lodged` IS A DATE
      `the day the objection was decided` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`s 23(3) -- the day the right to apply arose` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the right`
    WHEN `s 23(1)(a) -- no objection has been lodged` THEN `the day the notice was given`
    WHEN `s 23(2) -- the objection has been decided and written notice of the decision given` THEN `the day the objection was decided`
    OTHERWISE `s 23(1)(b) -- the days after lodging an objection before the right arises` `days after` `the day the objection was lodged`

-- s 23(3)(b): "more than 35 days ... after the objection was lodged": the first day an application can be made under
-- (1)(b) is the 36th day after lodging. Under (a) and (2) the first day is the day the right arose.
GIVEN `the right` IS A `The right to apply for review`
      `the day the notice was given` IS A DATE
      `the day the objection was lodged` IS A DATE
      `the day the objection was decided` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`s 23(3) -- the first day to apply` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the right`
    WHEN `s 23(1)(a) -- no objection has been lodged` THEN `the day the notice was given`
    WHEN `s 23(2) -- the objection has been decided and written notice of the decision given` THEN `the day the objection was decided`
    OTHERWISE (`s 23(1)(b) -- the days after lodging an objection before the right arises` PLUS 1) `days after` `the day the objection was lodged`

GIVEN `the right` IS A `The right to apply for review`
      `the day the notice was given` IS A DATE
      `the day the objection was lodged` IS A DATE
      `the day the objection was decided` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`s 23(3) -- the last day to apply` MEANS
    `s 23(3) -- the days to apply after the right arose` `days after` (`s 23(3) -- the day the right to apply arose` `the right` `the day the notice was given` `the day the objection was lodged` `the day the objection was decided`)

-- The permission, from the day the right arose. DO for the reason given at s 22. When the time runs out the Act
-- leaves nothing; the State Administrative Tribunal Act 2004 s 92 (deposited, not encoded) lets the Tribunal's rules
-- provide for extending a time limit, even after it has passed, so the person may ask.
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Graffiti party` `Graffiti act`
`s 23(3) -- the affected person may apply for review within 42 days after the right to apply arose` MEANS
    PARTY `the affected person`
    DO    `apply to the State Administrative Tribunal for a review`
    WITHIN `s 23(3) -- the days to apply after the right arose`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST  PARTY `the affected person`
          MAY   `ask the State Administrative Tribunal to extend the time for applying`

-- s 24(1): "If an objection has been lodged against a notice or a decision, or an application has been made under this
-- Division for a review of the decision, the effect of the notice or decision is suspended until the person or
-- tribunal authorised to deal with the objection or application has decided how to dispose of it, unless -- (a) the
-- person or tribunal orders that the suspension be revoked; or (b) the local government considers that -- (i) there
-- are urgent reasons ...; or (ii) suspension ... is reasonably likely to endanger the safety of any person, cause
-- damage to property or to create a serious public nuisance." s 24(3): the local government is to give the affected
-- person written reasons for a (1)(b) decision. Both exceptions are someone else's judgment: inputs.
GIVEN `an objection has been lodged or an application for review made` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the objection or application has been decided` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person or tribunal dealing with it has ordered the suspension revoked` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the local government considers there are urgent reasons, or a danger to people, property or the public` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 24(1) -- the effect of the notice is suspended` IF
        `an objection has been lodged or an application for review made`
    AND (NOT `the objection or application has been decided`)
    AND (NOT `the person or tribunal dealing with it has ordered the suspension revoked`)
    AND (NOT `the local government considers there are urgent reasons, or a danger to people, property or the public`)

-- The suspension as a rule: from the day the objection or application is lodged until it is decided, the local
-- government must not act on the notice by obliterating the graffiti under s 19(3). L4 cannot end a prohibition at
-- another party's act, so the days until the decision are a parameter: the rule's WITHIN. After the decision the
-- notice has effect again, or not, according to the decision (the Act does not say how its time then runs: NOTES.md
-- finding 4).
GIVEN `the person or tribunal dealing with it has ordered the suspension revoked` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the local government considers there are urgent reasons, or a danger to people, property or the public` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the days from lodging to the decision` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Graffiti party` `Graffiti act`
`s 24(1) -- the notice is suspended until the objection or application is decided` MEANS
    IF `s 24(1) -- the effect of the notice is suspended` TRUE FALSE `the person or tribunal dealing with it has ordered the suspension revoked` `the local government considers there are urgent reasons, or a danger to people, property or the public`
    THEN PARTY `the local government`
         SHANT `obliterate the graffiti`
         WITHIN `the days from lodging to the decision`
         HENCE FULFILLED
         LEST  BREACH BY `the local government` BECAUSE "s 24(1): while an objection or application for review is undecided, the effect of the notice is suspended, unless the suspension is revoked or the local government considers there are urgent reasons or a danger (s 24(1)(a), (b))"
    ELSE FULFILLED

§§ `s 25 Local government graffiti powers on land not local government property`

-- s 25(1): "A local government may obliterate graffiti that is visible from a public place and that has been applied
-- without the consent of the owner or occupier, even though the land on which it is done is not local government
-- property and the local government does not have consent to do it." This is not a notice case: s 18 covers graffiti
-- applied with consent. Entering the land still needs Division 4 (ss 26-29).
GIVEN `the graffiti was applied with the consent of the owner or occupier` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the graffiti is visible from a public place` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 25(1) -- the local government may obliterate the graffiti without consent` IF
        `the graffiti is visible from a public place`
    AND (NOT `the graffiti was applied with the consent of the owner or occupier`)

-- Where s 25(1) does not apply the Act gives no such power, and states nothing to forbid: nothing is owed.
GIVEN `the graffiti was applied with the consent of the owner or occupier` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the graffiti is visible from a public place` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Graffiti party` `Graffiti act`
`s 25(1) -- the local government may obliterate graffiti applied without consent` MEANS
    IF `s 25(1) -- the local government may obliterate the graffiti without consent` `the graffiti was applied with the consent of the owner or occupier` `the graffiti is visible from a public place`
    THEN PARTY `the local government`
         MAY   `obliterate the graffiti`
    ELSE FULFILLED

§§ `Part 3 Division 4 -- ss 26-30 Powers of entry`

-- s 27(1): "Except if the entry is authorised by the warrant of a justice, entry by or on behalf of a local government
-- on to any land, premises or thing is not lawful unless -- (a) the consent of the owner or occupier has been obtained;
-- or (b) notice has been given under section 28." s 27(2): after a s 28 notice, entry without consent is lawful
-- "unless the owner or occupier or a person authorised by the owner or occupier objects to the entry". s 28(3): "The
-- notice is to be given not less than 24 hours before the power of entry is exercised." s 29: a justice may issue a
-- warrant where entry is refused, opposed or cannot be had; it lasts until its purpose is satisfied or one month has
-- passed, whichever is shorter (s 29(3)(c)): an input, whether it is still in effect.
GIVEN `the owner or occupier has consented to the entry` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a justice's warrant authorises the entry and is still in effect` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `ss 27(1), 29 -- the entry is authorised by consent or a warrant` IF
        `a justice's warrant authorises the entry and is still in effect`
    OR  `the owner or occupier has consented to the entry`

`s 28(3) -- the hours of notice to be given before entry` MEANS 24

-- This rule counts in minutes from the moment the notice of entry is given. On the stable l4 an act at exactly the
-- end of a WITHIN is still within it, and s 28(3) allows entry once 24 hours have passed ("not less than 24 hours
-- before"), so the prohibition runs for the 24 hours less their last minute. An entry in the 1 440th minute or later
-- is allowed; anything earlier is not.
`s 28(3) -- the minutes after the notice during which entry is not allowed` MEANS
    (`s 28(3) -- the hours of notice to be given before entry` TIMES 60) MINUS 1

GIVEN `the owner or occupier has consented to the entry` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a justice's warrant authorises the entry and is still in effect` IS A BOOLEAN
      `notice of the intended entry has been given to the owner or occupier` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the owner or occupier, or a person they authorised, objects to the entry` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Graffiti party` `Graffiti act`
`ss 27, 28 -- the local government may enter only with consent, a warrant, or at least 24 hours' notice` MEANS
    IF `ss 27(1), 29 -- the entry is authorised by consent or a warrant` `the owner or occupier has consented to the entry` `a justice's warrant authorises the entry and is still in effect`
    THEN PARTY `the local government`
         MAY   `enter the land`
    ELSE IF     `notice of the intended entry has been given to the owner or occupier`
            AND (NOT `the owner or occupier, or a person they authorised, objects to the entry`)
         THEN PARTY `the local government`
              SHANT `enter the land`
              WITHIN `s 28(3) -- the minutes after the notice during which entry is not allowed`
              HENCE (PARTY `the local government` MAY `enter the land`)
              LEST  BREACH BY `the local government` BECAUSE "s 28(3): notice of an intended entry is to be given not less than 24 hours before the power of entry is exercised"
         ELSE PARTY `the local government`
              SHANT `enter the land`
              LEST  BREACH BY `the local government` BECAUSE "s 27(1), (2): entry by or on behalf of a local government is not lawful without the consent of the owner or occupier, a notice under s 28 (with no objection to the entry), or a justice's warrant"

-- s 30: "A person who enters or who has entered any land, premises or thing on behalf of a local government is to give
-- particulars of the power by virtue of which the local government claims a right of entry on being requested to do
-- so." No time is set: a function.
GIVEN `the person entering has been asked for the particulars` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 30 -- the person entering is to give particulars of the power of entry` IF
    `the person entering has been asked for the particulars`
